            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Cortés and the Aztec Empire</strong><br>\nCortés reached the Aztec capital of Tenochtitlán in 1519, where he was initially received as a guest by the Aztec emperor, Moctezuma II. This hospitality was based on a mixture of intrigue, Aztec diplomatic customs, and perhaps the belief in an old prophecy about a white-skinned god arriving from the east. Cortés took advantage of this situation to take control.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Capture of Moctezuma</strong><br>\nIn an audacious move, Cortés captured Moctezuma II and used him to control Tenochtitlán. However, this caused great unrest among the Aztec people. Eventually, the Spaniards were driven out of the city in a violent uprising known as La Noche Triste, or the \"Sad Night.\" During this chaos, Moctezuma was killed, leaving the Aztecs to elect a new emperor, Cuauhtémoc.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Siege and Fall of Tenochtitlán</strong><br>\nAfter regrouping and receiving reinforcements, Cortés laid siege to Tenochtitlán in 1521. The siege lasted several months, with the Spaniards cutting off the city's supplies and destroying its aqueducts. Despite fierce resistance, the Aztec capital fell to Cortés' forces. This marked a pivotal moment in the Spanish conquest of Mexico.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
