          },
          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Governor of New Spain</strong><br>\nFollowing his victory, Cortés was appointed governor and captain-general of the newly named territory of New Spain. As governor, he oversaw the rebuilding of Tenochtitlán, renaming it Mexico City, which became the center of Spanish power in the New World. His rule established the basis for the spread of European culture and the Spanish language in the Americas.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Later Years and Legacy</strong><br>\nCortés' later years were less triumphant. He faced legal challenges to his authority and spent much time defending his actions and territory in court. He continued to explore, but his power and wealth diminished. Cortés died in Spain in 1547, leaving behind a controversial legacy as both a skilled leader and a ruthless conqueror.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Historical Impact of Cortés</strong><br>\nHernán Cortés' conquests had a lasting impact on the world, leading to the spread of Spanish culture and language throughout the Americas. His actions also resulted in the devastation of indigenous populations, both through warfare and the introduction of European diseases. The story of Cortés is a powerful example of the complex consequences of European exploration and colonization.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
